  "knowledgePattern": "Compare integral values by matching intervals and ordering heights",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Put the graphs on comparable intervals",
      "body": "<p>Use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>cos</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>sin</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> and the angle-shift identities. For an expression involving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>, shift its interval by the same amount.</p>",
      "recap": "Moving a graph sideways does not change the area of the corresponding region.",
      "pitfall": "The square root of sin²x is |sin x|.",
      "pause": "Rewrite the expressions so you can compare their shapes.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b3-l08",
          "reminder": "Move the limits with the graph when using a translation."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Compare heights instead of integrating",
      "body": "<p>For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, compare <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msqrt><mi>u</mi></msqrt></mrow></math>. Apply your ordering to the nonnegative trig heights.</p>",
      "recap": "A graph that stays higher on the same interval has greater area.",
      "pitfall": "Check separately for any signed areas that cancel across zero.",
      "pause": "Use the graph heights and symmetry to identify the largest integral.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p2-b2-l06",
          "reminder": "The interval matters when comparing a number with its square or square root."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p><strong>1.</strong> A is zero: the real cube root of sine is odd, so its signed contributions cancel over the symmetric limits. For B, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>sin</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>. For E, the principal root is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>cos</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>sin</mi><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>. Thus B and E both integrate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>sin</mi><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>2.</strong> On the interior where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>sin</mi><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, squaring reduces the value. Therefore C, which integrates <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>sin</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, is strictly smaller than B and E.</p><p><strong>3.</strong> Translate the input in D by writing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. Its limits become <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, and its integrand is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>sin</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>. On <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>sin</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, a square root increases the value, so D is strictly larger than B and E.</p><p><strong>4.</strong> B and E are positive, while A is zero; therefore D is greatest. This compares graph heights and does not need antiderivatives of the trigonometric expressions.</p>"
}
